course: books
lesson: 91
title: Just So Stories, The Crab that Played with the Sea, part 2: Pau Amma loses his shell
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The little girl-daughter speaks up: at the Very Beginning she saw one beast slip away into the sea before he was taught his play. She describes him: round and flat, eyes on stalks, walking sideways, with strong armour on his back. «Now I know where Pau Amma went,» says the Magician.
explain: The sea carries the canoe to Pusat Tasek, the Heart of the Sea, a great hollow where the Wonderful Tree grows. The Magician reaches down and touches Pau Amma's back, and the whole sea rises, as water rises in a basin when you put your hand in. Pau Amma says he goes out to feed once a day and once a night, and the sea pours in and out after him.
explain: Hearing how important he is, Pau Amma laughs and threatens to go out seven times a day. He rises up: a King Crab so big that his shell touches Sarawak on one side and Pahang on the other. But when the Magician asks for a Magic, Pau Amma can only stir up the sea.
explain: The Magician makes a Magic with just the little finger of his left hand, and Pau Amma's shell falls off like a husk off a coconut. Soft and ashamed, he begs for his shell back. The Magician, the Man and the girl each offer a gift, but Pau Amma will not choose until he has his shell again.
fact: The girl describes Pau Amma: «He was round and he was flat; and his eyes grew upon stalks; and he walked sideways like this; and he was covered with strong armour upon his back.»
fact: The Magician says: «How wise are little children who see and are silent!» and then «How wise are little children who speak truth!»
fact: Pusat Tasek is «the Heart of the Sea», a great hollow leading to the heart of the world; there grows the Wonderful Tree, Pauh Janggi, with «magic twin nuts».
fact: When the Magician touches Pau Amma he «settled down», and «all the Sea rose up as water rises in a basin when you put your hand into it».
fact: Pau Amma: «Once a day and once a night I go out to look for my food. Once a day and once a night I return. Leave me alone.»
fact: When Pau Amma goes out, the beaches are left bare, little fish die and Raja Moyang Kaban, King of the Elephants, gets muddy legs; when he comes back, islands are drowned, the Man's house floods and Raja Abdullah, King of the Crocodiles, gets a mouthful of salt water.
fact: Pau Amma: «I did not know I was so important. Henceforward I will go out seven times a day.» (Henceforward = from now on.)
fact: Pau Amma is a King Crab, so big that «One side of his great shell touched the beach at Sarawak; the other touched the beach at Pahang», and he was taller than the smoke of three volcanoes.
fact: The girl picks out the soft eyes of the twin nut «with her little golden scissors».
fact: Soft Pau Amma cannot leave Pusat Tasek, because sharks and dogfish would eat him. The Magician will not simply give the shell back, because Pau Amma might grow big and proud again and forget his promise.
fact: The gifts: the Magician offers to make every stone, hole and bunch of weed a safe Pusat Tasek; the Man offers a home on land and in the sea; the girl offers her scissors, to eat coconuts, dig holes and climb trees.
fact: Real life: crabs have their skeleton on the outside; to grow, they climb out of the old shell (this is called moulting) and stay soft for a few days until the new one hardens.
fact: Real life: crabs do have eyes on stalks and most walk sideways, because their legs bend best that way. Their pincers are called claws or chelae; Kipling calls them «scissors».
fact: Real life: the coco de mer, a huge double nut from the Seychelles islands, was once found floating in the Indian Ocean; sailors who never saw the tree believed it grew under the sea, like Pauh Janggi.
fact: Real life: putting your hand in a basin does make the water rise, because your hand pushes water out of the way; but that is not what makes real tides, which come mostly from the Moon's pull.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «bruv» (London) = mate; «our kid» (Manchester) = my brother or sister; «toque» (Canada) = woolly hat.
exercise: Who tells the Magician about the beast that ran away? | The little girl-daughter | The Man | The Turtle | The Rat of the Moon, from high up in the sky
exercise: How does she describe Pau Amma? | Round and flat, eyes on stalks, walking sideways, with armour | Long and thin, with a curly tail, walking forwards on many legs | Big and grey with a trunk | Small and spotty, with wings
exercise: What does the Magician say when she tells him? | «How wise are little children who speak truth!» | «Why did you not tell me at once, at the Very Beginning, when it mattered?» | «Off you go to bed» | «Fetch my paddle»
exercise: Why is there no need to paddle? | The water flows past the islands by itself | The Magician lifts the canoe up into the air and flies it over the sea | The Man paddles very fast | A whale pulls it
exercise: What is Pusat Tasek? | The Heart of the Sea | A small island off Singapore where the Man keeps his canoe | A muddy river in Perak | A kind of big sea crab
exercise: What grows in Pusat Tasek? | The Wonderful Tree, Pauh Janggi, with magic twin nuts | A forest of seaweed taller than the smoke of three volcanoes | Coral | Nothing
exercise: What happens when the Magician touches Pau Amma's back? | Pau Amma settles and the sea rises | Pau Amma bites his hand so hard that the Magician pulls it back and shouts | The sea turns into ice | The Moon goes dark
exercise: Where does Pau Amma say he goes once a day and once a night? | Out to look for his food | Up to the Moon in the sky | Into the muddy Perak river | To sleep in a cave far under the sea
exercise: Who gets muddy legs when the water pours out? | Raja Moyang Kaban, King of the Elephants | Raja Abdullah, King of the Crocodiles | The Man | The Rat
exercise: Who gets a mouthful of salt water when the sea rises? | Raja Abdullah, King of the Crocodiles | Raja Moyang Kaban, King of the Elephants | The Fisherman | The girl
exercise: How does Pau Amma feel on hearing how important he is? | He laughs, pleased with himself | He is sorry and promises to stay at home in Pusat Tasek for ever | He cries | He runs away
exercise: «Henceforward I will go out seven times a day.» What does henceforward mean? | From now on | Forwards | Once more | Never
exercise: How big is Pau Amma when he rises? | His shell touches Sarawak and Pahang | As big as a dinner-plate, no more, like the crabs you find on the beach | As big as the canoe | As big as an island
exercise: What does the girl do with the twin nut? | Picks out its soft eyes with her little golden scissors | Throws it back into the deep sea | Gives it to the Magician | Plants it in the canoe
exercise: What can Pau Amma do when asked to make a Magic? | Only stir up the sea | Make a whole new island | Call the Moon down to him | Make himself invisible
exercise: With what does the Magician make his Magic? | Just the little finger of his left hand | All five fingers of his right hand, and a long word | His paddle | His breath
exercise: What happens to Pau Amma's shell? | It falls off like a husk off a coconut | It turns gold and shines in the moonlight like a great round mirror | It grows bigger | It breaks into twenty pieces
exercise: Why won't the Magician give the shell straight back? | Pau Amma might grow big and proud again and forget his promise | The shell is lost | He wants it for himself | It is broken
exercise: Why can't soft Pau Amma leave Pusat Tasek? | Sharks and dogfish would eat him | He is too sleepy after his long day of playing with the sea | The tree holds him | He has no legs
exercise: What does the Man offer? | A home in the sea and on dry ground | A canoe of his own, with a paddle, so he can go anywhere he likes | His kris | Food every day
exercise: What does the girl offer? | Her scissors, to crack nuts, dig holes and climb trees | Her shell bracelets | A song | Half her nut
exercise: Looking back: in part 1, what did the Man ask the Magician? | To make all the Animals obedient to him | To teach him a play of his own, like the Elephant and the Beaver | To give him a canoe | To make him lazy
test: Jay from Peckham: «the Magician knew where the crab was all along, bruv.» (bruv = mate) Put it right. | He only knew after the girl described the beast | Right, he could see right through the deep water to the bottom | The Rat told him | The Moon told him
test: The Magician praises children «who see and are silent», then «who speak truth». Why both? | She noticed quietly, then told the truth when it was needed | She was silent all through the story, and the Magician guessed everything himself | She lied first | She sang
test: Why does the sea rise when the Magician puts his arm in and Pau Amma settles? | Like a hand in a basin, the big body pushes water up | The Moon pulls it | It is raining | The tree drinks it
test: Pau Amma threatens to go out seven times a day. What would that mean, in the story? | The sea would never be still | Better fishing for the Man, because the fish would come up the river more often | More sleep | Nothing at all
test: Which is TRUE in this part? | Pau Amma can only stir up the sea, though he is a King Crab | Pau Amma makes a great Magic | The Magician uses his right hand | The girl is afraid
test: Real life: how do real crabs grow? | They moult and stay soft for a while | They slowly stretch the old shell a little bigger each year, like a jumper | They never grow | A magician changes them
test: Real life: why do most crabs walk sideways? | Their legs bend best that way | They are shy and do not like to look anyone in the face | They cannot see anything in front | They are always dizzy from the waves
test: Real life: what nut did sailors once think grew under the sea? | The coco de mer | The peanut, because it grows under the ground and looks like a little shell | The acorn of the oak | The sweet chestnut
test: Ruby from Manchester: «our kid says putting your hand in the bath makes the tide.» (our kid = my brother) Put it right. | A hand raises bath water, but tides come from the Moon's pull | Right, that's exactly how tides work | Tides come from crabs | Tides come from wind only
test: Looking back: in the Elephant's Child, who helped pull against the Crocodile? | The Bi-Coloured-Python-Rock-Snake | Kolokolo Bird, who flew down from the fever-trees | The Ostrich | The Hippopotamus
test: Looking back: which earlier character also boasted and was put in his place, being made «different» in a way he did not like? | Old Man Kangaroo | Little Taffy herself | The slow Tortoise | The muddy Stranger-man
test: Looking back: which earlier animal could take off his skin and button it up again? | The Rhinoceros | The Camel, who took off his hump at night to rest it | The spotted Leopard | The great Whale
test: Looking back: in part 1, which animal's flung sand became Borneo and Java? | All-the-Turtle-there-was | All-the-Cow-there-was | All-the-Beaver-there-was | All-the-Elephant-there-was
test: Amir from Toronto, in his toque (woolly hat): «why didn't the crab just take the gifts straight off?» What did Pau Amma say? | All soft as he was, the gifts would not help him; first his shell | He didn't want any gifts | He took them all at once | He fell asleep
test: ★★★ «Once a day and once a night I go out... Once a day and once a night I return.» How many times does the sea rise and fall in a day and a night, by this? | It goes out twice and comes in twice | Once out, once in | Seven times | Not at all
test: ★★★ Counting: how many kings are named in this part, Pau Amma included? | Three | Two: the King of the Elephants and the King of the Crocodiles, as the crab is not a real king | One: Pau Amma | Four, with the Man
test: ★★★ The Magician praised children «who see and are silent». What had the girl seen at the Very Beginning and kept quiet about? | Pau Amma slipping away into the sea | The Rat biting the line | The Magician's little finger | The sharks
exam: Identify the description of the escaped beast furnished by the little girl-daughter. | Round and flat, with eyes upon stalks, walking sideways, armoured upon the back | Long and thin with a coiled tail | Grey and woolly with four short legs | Spotted and winged
exam: Ascertain the consequence of the Magic made with the little finger of the Magician's left hand. | Pau Amma's shell fell from him as a husk from a coconut | Pau Amma was enlarged to such a degree that his shell extended from Sarawak to Pahang | The sea froze | The Moon descended
exam: It is given that Pau Amma, rendered soft, declined to leave Pusat Tasek. Select the reason most consistent with his own words. | He feared that sharks and dogfish would devour him | He was too large to move | He wished to remain near the Tree | He was asleep
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the growth of real crabs? | They moult their old shell and remain soft until a new one hardens | They grow within an unchanging shell | They never moult | They are given new shells by magic
